\f1\fs22 In the 
\f3\i Memphis Mafia Bar
\f1\i0 , a waiter called \cf0 Lonnie\cf2  greeted Tony with a hug, a kiss and a grope, and showed them to a centre-room booth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah ordered, \'91We\'92ll have something Airborne and bubbly.\'92\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie\cf2  snickered and yielded an Elvi-brand lopsided smile. \'91You\'92re a long way from the tourist drive, sweetie. I\'92ll get you something nice,\'92 she said and pat Sarah\'92s shoulder. Her eyes twinkled as she transferred a pitcher of Choke cola, a bowl of peanuts, a jar of peanut butter and deep-fried crackers from her tray to the table.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> Sarah pulled her tablet from her bag, swiped and gestured over its surface. Tony pitched her a look with a three-storey question mark glued to it. \'91I\'92m scanning their security,\'92 she explained. \'91We must be careful from now on.\'92 Sarah beelined to a corner, pat the drapes, and located an ancient microphone and transmitter. In the opposite corner she discovered a crooked old camera.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On squeaking back to the booth, she told Tony, \'91I think we\'92re safe.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On stage, an Elvi impersonator was singing a tune that more wholesome universes would recognise as 
\f3\i A Little Less Conversation
\f1\i0 . Unfortunately, the impersonator\'92s performance was provoking a little more conversation, as well as boos and catcalls, among the genuine Elvi\'97the real ELVIS units. Security escorted the non-Elvi from the stage to the alley door.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sorry about that,\'92 \cf0 Lonnie\cf2  said when she returned with a beer for Tony and an elaborate fruit-salad cocktail for Sarah. \'91Tourist venturing too far into the Ghetto. I don\'92t why they try that. Don\'92t they know it\'92s cultural appropriation?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah grabbed the beer and guzzled it. \cf0 Lonnie\cf2  returned within seconds to deliver a fresh one. Tony meekly sipped the cocktail, and felt his eyeballs crystallising with sugar. He pushed the cocktail aside.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah opened her purse and retrieved something wrapped in tissues. She shelled the disc from the paper and placed on the table, like she was going all-in at high-stakes poker.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You brought that thing?\'92 Even Tony could see it was grotty from lying around in his van for weeks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What the hell 
\f3\i is
\f1\i0  this?\'92 Sarah said, her finger pinning the disc.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How should I know?\'92 Tony defended. Somehow, the cocktail glass jumped into his hand and poured liquid inflammation down his throat. He thought he could taste bacon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A PAPER agent wants to kill us for this!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You think it\'92s my fault?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No.\'92 Sarah deflated. \'91It\'92s my fault. I\'92m the one who set that psychopath onto you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hey, it\'92s OK.\'92 Tony reached for Sarah\'92s hand. \'91I set him onto you too. If you hadn\'92t been taking care of me in my apartment when he called, you wouldn\'92t be in his sights.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tax!\'92 Sarah eyes widened with realisation. \'91You can\'92t go home!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We\'92ll worry about that later.\'92 Tony slid the disc out from under Sarah\'92s hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A rainbow sheen slithered over the disc. \'91What the Jesus Taxing Christ?\'92 Sarah blurted. Tony looked stunned. \'91And don\'92t tell me it\'92s the light!\'92 Sarah was flustered, which frightened him. He could say nothing while she called someone she knew. \'91Johnny,\'92 she explained to Tony. \'91Tech genius.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny answered the call. She demanded he come meet her \'85 no he wouldn\'92t catch cooties, and this bar was much cleaner than last time\'97well, tidier\'85no maybe it was the lighting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92ll have to sit somewhere else,\'92 Sarah told Tony. \'91I can\'92t let Johnny see me with a Cloudborne.\'92\
